course: everyday
lesson: 33
title: Medicines and cleaning products
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A medicine is a chemical that helps the body when the right amount is taken. Too much of it, or the wrong one, can be a poison.
explain: That is why only a parent, a carer, a nurse or a doctor gives you medicine, and they read the label for the right dose. Medicine is never a sweet, even when it tastes like one.
explain: Cleaning products are strong chemicals made to kill germs and dissolve dirt. They can burn your mouth, eyes and skin, and some give off harmful fumes when they are mixed.
explain: For example, bleach mixed with some other cleaners makes a poisonous gas. That is why cleaners are never mixed, and why you never sniff or taste a bottle to find out what is inside.
explain: If someone may have swallowed something harmful, an adult calls a poison help line at once, such as 1-800-222-1222 in the USA or 13 11 26 in Australia. If the person is very sleepy, cannot breathe well or will not wake, call the emergency number.
explain: A toddler in Texas found her grandma's pills in a handbag and thought they were sweets; she swallowed several and had to be rushed to hospital.
explain: A little boy in Liverpool bit into a laundry detergent pod that looked like a toy; it burst in his mouth and he had to be treated in hospital for burns and breathing problems.
fact: Medicine should be taken only when a trusted adult gives it to you.
fact: Taking more than the right dose of a medicine can be very dangerous.
fact: Some medicines look and taste like sweets, but they are not sweets.
fact: You should never taste, sniff or drink anything from an unknown bottle or container.
fact: Cleaning products can burn the mouth, throat, skin and eyes.
fact: Different cleaning products must never be mixed, because some mixtures give off poisonous gas.
fact: Chemicals should always stay in their own labelled bottles, never in drink bottles or cups.
fact: Many medicine and cleaner bottles have special caps that are hard to open, to stop young children getting into them.
fact: In the USA, the Poison Help line is 1-800-222-1222; in Australia, the Poisons Information Centre is 13 11 26.
fact: If someone has swallowed a poison and has trouble breathing or will not wake up, you call the emergency number.
fact: You should not try to make someone vomit after swallowing a poison unless an expert tells you to, because a strong chemical can burn the throat again on the way back up.
fact: Small round button batteries are very dangerous if swallowed and need a doctor straight away.
fact: Laundry pods and dishwasher tablets are poisonous and must be kept away from small children.
fact: Many children are poisoned every year by medicines or cleaning products they found at home.
fact: A swallowed button battery can burn through the throat in about two hours, so it is an emergency.
exercise: Who should give you medicine? | A parent, carer, nurse or doctor | Any friend who has the same illness | Yourself, if you know where it is kept | An older child at school
exercise: A bottle of cough medicine tastes of strawberries. Is it all right to drink some as a treat? | No, medicine is never a treat and too much can harm you | Yes, if it tastes nice it cannot hurt you | Yes, as long as you only drink half a bottle of it at one time | No, because it is only for adults with colds
exercise: You find a bottle with no label under the sink. What should you do? | Leave it alone and tell an adult | Sniff it to find out what it is | Taste a tiny drop to check | Pour it out down the sink to be safe
exercise: Why must cleaning products never be mixed together? | Some mixtures make a poisonous gas | Mixing them makes them stop cleaning | Mixing them always makes them explode | They turn into water and are wasted
exercise: A boy in Texas sees cleaner poured into an old lemonade bottle. Why is that dangerous? | Someone could think it is a drink and swallow it | The cleaner will turn into lemonade | The bottle will make the cleaner weaker so it stops working | Cleaners only work in their own bottles
exercise: What is the Poison Help line number in the USA? | 1-800-222-1222 | 1-800-123-4567 | 1-911-911-0000 | 1-800-555-0100
exercise: What number do people in Australia call for poison advice? | 13 11 26 | 13 00 00 | 11 22 33 | 10 10 10
exercise: A toddler has swallowed some dishwasher liquid and is struggling to breathe. What should be done? | Call the emergency number at once | Wait an hour to see if it gets better | Give the toddler more liquid to wash it down | Put the toddler to bed to sleep it off
exercise: Why should you not make someone vomit after they swallow a cleaner? | The chemical can burn the throat again on the way up | Vomiting makes the cleaner much stronger inside the stomach | It is rude to make people vomit | Vomiting makes the poison turn into gas
exercise: What can happen if a person swallows a small button battery? | It can burn inside their body and needs a doctor at once | Nothing, because it passes out harmlessly in a day or two | It recharges itself inside their stomach | It only causes a mild tummy ache
exercise: Why are laundry pods dangerous for little children? | They look like sweets but contain strong chemicals | They are too big to fit in a child's mouth | They only become harmful after washing clothes | They are made of sugar and rot teeth
exercise: Can taking twice the dose of a medicine make you better twice as fast? | No, too much medicine can be poisonous | Yes, more medicine always works faster | Yes, but only with pills, not syrups | No, but it is always harmless to try
exercise: Where should cleaning products be kept at home? | Up high or locked away in their own bottles | On the kitchen table where they are easy to find | In old drink bottles to save space | In the fridge next to the juice
test: A girl in Belfast feels ill and sees her mum's pills in a drawer. Her mum is at the shops. What should she do? | Wait and ask her mum or another trusted adult | Take one of the pills to feel better until her mum gets back | Take two pills so they work faster | Ask a friend which pill to try
test: Which statement is true? | Medicine can harm you if you take the wrong amount | Medicine is safe in any amount because it heals | Medicine that tastes sweet is just a sweet | Only pills can be harmful, not syrups
test: Dad is cleaning the bathroom and wants to add a second cleaner to the bleach to make it stronger. What should you tell him? | Mixing cleaners can make poisonous gas | That will make the bathroom cleaner faster | Two cleaners together turn into water | Bleach only works when it is mixed
test: A boy in Brisbane finds his baby sister chewing a dishwasher tablet. She seems fine. What should the family do? | Phone the Poisons Information Centre on 13 11 26 | Do nothing, because she seems fine | Give her lots of sweets to take the taste away | Wait until morning to see a doctor
test: Why is an old water bottle a bad place to keep weed killer? | Someone could drink it, thinking it is water | Weed killer goes off faster in plastic | The bottle will melt in a few hours | Weed killer must be kept in a jar of sand
test: Which of these should you do if you find a strange powder in a bag at the park? | Leave it, do not touch it and tell an adult | Taste a little to see if it is just sugar or salt | Take it home to show your friends | Sniff it to find out what it is
test: What would most likely happen if someone sprayed a strong cleaner near their eyes? | It could burn and hurt their eyes | It would make their eyes see more clearly | Nothing, because cleaners are safe for skin | It would only make their eyes feel cool
test: A child swallowed some pills and now cannot be woken up. What is the right action? | Call the emergency number straight away | Call a friend and ask what to do | Try to make the child vomit | Let the child sleep it off and check on them later
test: Why do medicine bottles often have special caps that are hard to open? | To stop young children from opening them | To keep the medicine warm inside | To make the medicine last longer in the bottle | To stop adults from taking too much
test: mum these look like sweeties in the cupboard | Don't eat them, they may be medicine | They're probably sweets, try one | Just eat a small one | Ask your little brother to try one first
test: found a bottle with no label, gonna sniff it | No, never taste or sniff unknown stuff | Yes, sniffing is safe | Taste a tiny drop instead | Shake it first and then smell it
test: baby put a button battery in his mouth | Get a doctor straight away | Wait and see | Give him some water | It's fine, batteries are tiny
test: can i put cleaner in this old drinks bottle | No, keep chemicals in their own labelled bottles | Yes, it's easier to pour | Yes, if you rinse it out first | Yes, if you write on it later with a pen what it is
test: lil bro just swallowed a button battery from the remote!! | Tell an adult and get emergency help right now | Give him bread to push it down | Wait until it comes out on its own | Make him jump to bring it up
test: found pills in mum's bag, look like sweets, can i try one | Never, give them to an adult, they could poison you | Yes, one cannot hurt | Yes, if they are bright | Yes, if you are feeling ill and mum isn't around to ask
test: Zara says bleach in a juice bottle is fine if u label it. true? | No, someone could drink it, keep it in its own bottle | Yes, labels always stop mistakes, everyone reads them | Yes, if the lid is tight | Yes, unless it smells strong
test: did u hear a toddler ate his nan's tablets thinking they were sweets, he had to go to hospital | Real lesson: medicines can look like sweets, so only take medicine from a trusted adult | Real lesson: nans should not have any tablets | It proves sweets are always dangerous | It is gossip, so medicine is fine to taste
test: my mate says if u drink bleach u just gotta make yourself sick and ur fine | Wrong; do not make someone vomit unless an expert says so; call Poison Help or the emergency number | He is right, being sick fixes it every time | He is right, then drink lots of milk | He is right, but only for a small sip
test: someone says Ellie's mum takes loads of pills cos she's crazy, everyone's saying it | Do not spread it; it is cruel and private, and many people take medicine to stay well | Spread it, since pills prove she is unwell | Spread it, so people avoid Ellie | Spread it, but only to your friends
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the proper taking of medicine by a child? | Medicine should be taken only when administered by a trusted adult | Medicine may be taken whenever a child feels unwell | Medicine may be taken in a double dose for speed | Medicine resembling sweets may be eaten freely
exam: Identify the principal reason for which chemicals must remain in their own labelled bottles. | Chemicals placed in drink bottles or cups may be mistaken for drink | Labelled bottles cause the chemical to last longer | Chemicals lose their strength in other containers | Labels are required solely for recycling
exam: It being given that a person has swallowed a poison and is unable to breathe properly or to wake, select the appropriate action. | To call the emergency number at once | To induce vomiting without delay | To give the person a large meal | To allow the person to sleep it off undisturbed
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning cleaning products. | They may burn the mouth, throat, skin and eyes | They are harmless provided only a little is swallowed | They are dangerous only when heated | They cause harm only to small animals
